PART 2  does descent ON THE ESTIMATE actually reduce the loss?
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  v123 measured the cosine but STEPPED with the exact gradient, so it
  never showed the estimator driving its own trajectory. This does, and
  compares against exact-gradient descent from the same start.

    |D|   seed    MSE start   MSE end (est)   MSE end (exact)   verdict
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      8      0     0.99758       0.81520          0.82343        down
      8      1     1.20815       0.89614          0.89202        down
      8      2     1.34446       0.78567          0.76491        down

====================================================================================================
READING IT
====================================================================================================
  Circuits per epoch is 3 at every size - one register readout plus one
  per sign branch - and it is COUNTED, not asserted: q.ncircuits is
  incremented at each backend.run. Parameter-shift on the same problem
  is 2M = 24 per sample, 384 for the batch at |D|=16, so the ratio is 128x
  and it grows with both |D| and M.

  ON AVERAGE the estimator tracks the true MSE gradient at every size
  (worst mean cos +0.9063), and seed-averaging closes v123's open
  caveat: the non-monotone 0.9773 / 0.9035 / 0.9341 it saw across |D|
  was one draw. Averaged over 3 seeds the trend is monotone in |D|,
  which is what the shot model predicts and what should be quoted.

  THE MIN COLUMN IS NOT NOISE AROUND THE MEAN AND SHOULD NOT BE READ AS
  IT. Worst single epoch over all seeds and sizes: cos +0.2358.

  supplement/v124 identified the cause and it is SIGNAL-TO-NOISE, not the
  sign-branch split. cos is a ratio, so it collapses wherever the true
  gradient is small against the estimator's error floor: binning 240
  epochs by |g_true| quartile gives err/|g| of 0.89, 0.39, 0.26, 0.20 and
  mean cos of +0.7744, +0.8946, +0.9565, +0.9802. The min entries above
  are FLAT-REGION epochs. More shots where |g| is small is the fix.

  WITHDRAWN, and left here because it was wrong in an instructive way.
  An earlier version of this file blamed the tail on samples whose
  residual sits near zero being flipped into the wrong branch, and
  predicted the tail would worsen as training converged. v124's control
  arm logs ZERO flips across 120 epochs and still hits min cos -0.0752,
  which rules the mechanism out; and in its realizable arm flips do rise
  (0.00 -> 0.12/epoch) while cos IMPROVES (+0.9476 -> +0.9916), because a
  sample only flips when its residual - hence its gradient weight - is
  small. Misassigned weight mass peaks at 0.87%. The split protects
  itself; the prediction had the sign backwards.

  Descent on the estimate reduced the loss on 3/3 seeds, and landed
  within a few percent of exact-gradient descent from the same start -
  sometimes above it, sometimes below. So the lower tail above costs
  the TRAJECTORY little: momentum in the parameters averages over it in
  a way a single-epoch cosine cannot show. This is also the first time
  the estimator has driven its own trajectory in this line of work;
  v74, v121, v122 and v123 all stepped with something else.

  Both arms end near MSE 0.8 from a start near 1.0-1.3. That is the
  ansatz and the task, NOT the estimator - the exact-gradient arm does
  no better. Nothing here claims the model fits well; the claim is that
  three circuits buy the same trajectory the exact gradient buys.

  SCOPE. N_sys=3, |D| <= 16, efficient_su2 reps=1, 32768 shots, 3 seeds,
  no noise model, no hardware. PART 1 steps with the exact gradient so
  every seed sees the same trajectory; PART 2 steps with the estimate.
  The exact arms are tier B (Statevector) and are the REFERENCE - no
  headline number here rests on them.
